The '''Intelligence Service''' ({{lang-bl|Servicio de Inteligencia}}; '''SI'''), commonly known as '''IC3''', is the foreign {{W|intelligence service}} of the [[Baliscan Confederation]], tasked mainly with the covert overseas collection and analysis of [[W:human intelligence (intelligence collection)|human intelligence]] in support of Balisca's national security. SSI is one of the agencies of the [[Baliscan Intelligence Community]] and the [[Head of the Secret Intelligence Service]] ("H") is directly accountable to the [[Secretariat of International Affairs and Cooperation (Balisca)|International Affairs Secretary]].

Formed in 1914 as the foreign section of the '''National Intelligence Service''', the section grew greatly during the {{W|First World War}} officially adopting its current name around 1920. The name "IC3" (meaning Clandestine Intelligence, Section 3) originated as a convenient label following the [[Baliscan Revolution]], when SI was known by many names. It is still commonly used today. The size of the SI was greatly expanded during the 1976 [[South Atlantic War]]; the [[Intelligence Services Act]] was introduced to Congress, to place the organisation on a statutory footing for the first time, and provides the legal basis for its operations. Today, SI is subject to public oversight by the [[Investigatory Powers Council]] and the Congressional [[Intelligence and Security Committee (Balisca)|Intelligence and Security Committee]].

The stated priority roles of SI are {{W|counter-terrorism}}, [[W:Counter-proliferation|counter-proliferation]], providing intelligence in support of {{W|cyber security}}, and supporting stability overseas to disrupt terrorism and other criminal activities. Unlike its main sister agencies, [[Social Protection Service]] (SPS) and [[Joint Communications Headquarters]] (SCC), SI works exclusively in foreign intelligence gathering; the SCC allows it to carry out operations only against persons outside the of Baliscan territory.{{Balisca articles}}
